A||N||4||Soulsinger||Thermostat housing boil over||||1||
Z||000000||Soulsinger||03-20-2000||10:12 PM||||We have an 84 fiero with an 86 motor (2.5l). Just hand motor put in. We have water/anti-freeze coming out of the thermostat housing cap. We have replaced the cap and the thermostat. The car doesn't seem to overheat (now that we have the right belt on and it stays on). The temperature gauge runs between 175 and 220, when it reaches 220 it comes right back down. The idle is high, at start up about 3,000 rpms warm about 2,000. One person tells us we have a blown head gasket, but he also kept calling my car a Ferrari. Hmmmmm. No other leaks, no water in oil. Any help would be most appreciated. First Fiero owners here. By the way we did "burp" the cooling system today.||||reg||1
Z||000001||DRH||03-21-2000||02:13 PM||||How much water? Is it constant or change (more or less) as it warms up?<P>A blown head gasket is a possiblity, but there must be something else wrong too. As pressure builds up in the cooling system it should be relieved thru the radiator cap into the coolant resevoir. This happens under normal operation and should just happen much faster with a blown head gasket. It sounds like either the radiator cap is bad or the thermostat housing is bent and not making a good seal. If the housing looks OK I would replace the radiator cap.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||mwbackus||03-21-2000||02:51 PM||||Your description of the cooling system operation sounds just as it should be, I would assume you have no problem in this area.  The high idle IS a problem and it is very likely that a vacuum leak is responsible for this.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||03-21-2000||04:12 PM||||Sounds like your thermostat housing cap is not making a good seal.  My DD (dearly departed) 84 leaked coolant there until I took some fairly coarse sandpaper and sanded the lip of the housing smooth where it meets the seal on the cap (it was corroded).  No more leaks!||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||03-21-2000||07:14 PM||||I'd have to agree with Chad..... The T house cap is not a venting cap in any sense. It's more like a jar lid. If you already replaced the cap then it's got to be the T house. It could be the seal face. If the seal face is ok then it could also be the "ears" on the T house have worn down and the cap isn't tight enough. Best fix for that is replace the T house.<P>Coolant temp bouncing around if the fan is off is normal. With the fan on it should be pretty stable.<P>If you had a blown head gasket it wouldn't be hard to figure out. Fix the T house seal and if it boils over, but now up front, you have a problem you need to look at. It could be a blown gasket or a number of other things.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 03-21-2000).]||||reg||1
